@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "02. Build a 2x2 Tile Map") {
        Use SpriteKit's `SKTileMapNode`, `SKTileSet`, and `SKTileGroup` to build the smallest playable board: a 2x2 tile map.

        Language: [한국어](/2026TechMap_tutorial/CubeOneStrokePuzzleKO/tutorials/cube-one-stroke-puzzle-ko/02-spritekit-tiles-and-touch-names/).
    }

    @Section(title: "Prepare a tile palette") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            An `SKTileGroup` describes how one kind of tile should look. For example, empty, visited, current, and completed tiles can each have a different tile group.

            This project keeps the color rule simple. The current tile is `.blue`; visited tiles and every tile on a completed face are `.systemTeal`. A completed face is not replaced with one flat teal material; it stays a tile map with square cells and borders.

            An `SKTileSet` collects those tile groups into a palette. After the palette exists, each cell in an `SKTileMapNode` can point to one tile group from that palette.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create tile groups for empty, blocked, visited, current, and completed cells. Collect the tile groups in one `SKTileSet`, and add the helper that turns a filled `UIImage` into an `SKTileGroup`.

                @Code(name: "SpriteKitFaceScene.swift", file: "02-TilePalette.swift")

                @Image(source: preview-placeholder.svg, alt: "Preview placeholder for the screen after adding this code.")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Create the 2x2 grid") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `SKTileMapNode` is a grid node. Instead of drawing each square as a separate `SKShapeNode`, the tile map owns the rows and columns.

            This tutorial starts with 2x2 on purpose. Learn the SpriteKit tile map structure and the SceneKit surface-touch connection while the board is small, then expand the same structure to 3x3 later.

            On the cube face, titles, status text, and path lines make the surface harder to read. The final SpriteKit texture keeps only square tiles and coordinate labels.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the board metrics, tile map, tile label, tile role, and label color helpers. The `SKTileMapNode` uses `board.boardSize` for both columns and rows, then assigns one `SKTileGroup` to each cell.

                @Code(name: "SpriteKitFaceScene.swift", file: "02-TwoByTwoTileMap.swift")

                @Image(source: preview-placeholder.svg, alt: "Preview placeholder for the screen after adding this code.")
            }

            @Step {
                Do not add a path line here. Selection state is shown by tile color instead of by connecting strokes, so the changed code is all inside `drawTileMap`.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Connect the idea to tileID") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            If every tile were a separate node, you could read `node.name` to recover a name such as `tile_0_0`. With `SKTileMapNode`, the whole grid is one node, and each cell is identified by row and column instead.

            This project uses `TileID(row:column:)` as the tile address. Later, when a SceneKit cube face is tapped, `textureCoordinates` gives a 2D position from `0` to `1`, and that position becomes a `TileID` on the current 2x2 or 3x3 board.

            When a SpriteKit scene becomes a texture on a cube face, labels can appear mirrored or upside down depending on the face direction. Each stage therefore stores a `coordinateLayout` for touch-to-tile remapping and a `labelCorrection` for readable labels.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                For now, check the visible tile map. The precise 3D touch conversion is connected in the SceneKit chapter.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Check the result") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            You should see a 2x2 SpriteKit tile map. The different colors are a learning aid so each tile role is visible.

            In `SKTileMapNode`, row `0` is the bottom row. The labels such as `(0,0)` and `(0,1)` preview the `TileID` address each cell will use.

            @Image(source: expected-02-tilemap.svg, alt: "A 2x2 SKTileMapNode using SKTileGroup values from an SKTileSet palette.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Say the core relationship out loud: `SKTileGroup` is a tile appearance, `SKTileSet` is the palette, and `SKTileMapNode` is the grid.
            }
        }
    }
}
